<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sikandar Begum</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1817">1817</a>–<a href="/years/1868">1868</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sikandar-lodi">Sikandar</a> Begum was the ruler of Bhopal State, first as regent and from 1860 as begum in her own right, the second of the four women who governed Bhopal between 1819 and 1926. She reformed its revenue and administration, stayed loyal to the British during the rising of 1857 and in 1863 became the first Indian ruler to perform the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hajj">hajj</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sikandar-begum.jpg" alt="Sikandar Begum" width="264" height="475" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown; subject died 1868. · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sikandar_Begum_Bhopal.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Sikandar was born at Gauhar Mahal in Bhopal State on 10 September 1817, the daughter of Nasir Muhammad Khan and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qudsia-begum">Qudsia Begum</a>, both former rulers of the state. On 18 April 1835 she married Nawab Jahangir Mohammad Khan; their only child was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shah-jahan-begum">Shah Jahan Begum</a>. When the nine-year-old <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shah-jahan">Shah Jahan</a> came to the throne, Joseph Davey Cunningham, political agent of the Governor-General of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, announced Sikandar's appointment as regent, and the governor-general gave her the state's executive powers.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>During the rising of 1857 she banned anti-British pamphlets, strengthened her intelligence network and bribed rebellious soldiers to change sides. In August sepoys attacked British garrisons at Sehore and Berasia, and in December, encouraged by her mother, they surrounded her palace; she sent her son-in-law Umrao Daulah to negotiate and ended the siege by raising their pay. The British recognised her as Nawab of Bhopal on 30 September 1860, made her a Knight Grand Commander in 1861 and raised her salute to 19 guns.</p><p>In 1863 she performed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hajj">Hajj</a> with about 1,000 companions, mostly women, and wrote an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/urdu">Urdu</a> memoir of the journey, translated into English in 1870, in which she called <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mecca">Mecca</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jeddah">Jeddah</a> unclean and complained of Turkish customs officials. At home she divided the state into three districts and 21 sub-districts, repaid its debt and founded a customs office, a secretariat, a mint, a postal service and a modern judiciary with a court of appeal.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Sikandar founded the Victoria School for girls and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/urdu">Urdu</a> and Hindi middle schools in every district, set up a Majlis-e-Shoora of nobles and intellectuals to recommend laws, and in 1862 made Urdu the court language in place of Persian. She built the Moti Masjid of red sandstone and the Moti Mahal and Shaukat Mahal palaces. A devout Muslim who wore no face veil and kept no purdah, she hunted tigers, played polo and commanded the army. She died of kidney failure on 30 October 1868 and was succeeded by her daughter.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Sikandar Begum” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>From 1819 to 1926 four women ruled in turn: Qudsia Begum, <b>Sikandar Begum</b>, Shah Jahan Begum and Sultan Jahan Begum, who built mosques, schools, railways and the Taj-ul-Masajid.</blockquote><a href="/state/bhopal-state">Bhopal State</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Bhopal, in what is now <a href="/countries/india">India</a>. Died in Bhopal, in what is now <a href="/countries/india">India</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=23.26,77.41&amp;year=1850" aria-label="Bhopal on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6616 1306 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Sikandar Begum"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7066 1576h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7066 1576h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=23.26,77.41&amp;year=1850">What was here in 1850? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
